# rust-licenses.manifest — the licence files Astro supplies to vendored Rust
# crates whose published crates.io tarball ships none.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `tools/vendor-adblock-rust.sh` adds an `adblock` dependency to Chromium's
# `third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/Cargo.toml` and asks Chromium's own
# `gnrt` to vendor it and generate the `BUILD.gn` files the build needs.
# `gnrt gen` writes a `README.chromium` for every vendored crate, and refuses —
# for the whole run, not just that crate — when it cannot find a licence file
# in the crate's vendor directory:
#
#     License file not found for crate `convert_case-0.4.0`.
#
# Thirteen of the fifty-five crates the adblock dependency pulls in declare a
# licence in their `Cargo.toml` and ship no licence FILE in the tarball
# published to crates.io. That is a defect in those releases, not in gnrt: the
# text is present in each project's own repository and was simply not included
# in the packaged artifact.
#
# So the file is taken from the project's own repository, at a pinned commit,
# and installed into the vendor directory after `gnrt vendor` and before
# `gnrt gen`. Nothing is composed, edited or reformatted — each `source` below
# is a byte-exact copy, and its `sha256` is verified on every run, so a
# corrupted or substituted text stops the vendoring instead of reaching a
# `README.chromium` that would then misstate the terms.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE MUST NEVER DO
#
# Invent attribution. A licence text carrying a copyright line names a real
# person or organisation, and writing one that upstream did not write is a
# false claim shipped to every user. Every `source` here is therefore either
#
#   * a file that exists in the crate's upstream repository, taken verbatim at
#     the commit named in its `origin`; or
#   * the verbatim, unmodified text of the licence the crate's own Cargo.toml
#     declares, from the body that publishes it — used ONLY where upstream has
#     never had a licence file to take. The Apache-2.0 document names nobody
#     (its appendix is a bracketed template), so installing it asserts the
#     terms the crate itself declares and attributes nothing to anyone.
#
# HOW GNRT FINDS THE FILE
#
# `tools/crates/gnrt/lib/readme.rs` resolves the crate's SPDX expression to one
# `LicenseKind` — `Apache-2.0` wins over `MIT` in a dual expression, which is
# why the two dual-licensed crates below get the Apache text — and then tries a
# fixed list of filenames in the vendor directory: `LICENSE-<Kind>`, then
# `LICENSE-<KIND>`, then a bare `LICENSE`, each with an optional `.md`/`.txt`
# and a `LICENCE` spelling. The `dest` column below is a name from that list,
# so no `gnrt_config.toml` override is needed and Chromium's own tracked file
# is not touched.
#
# FORMAT
#
#   source  <path under tools/vendor/licenses/>  <sha256>  <origin URL>
#   install <vendor directory name>  <dest filename>  <source path>
#
# The join is strict in both directions and `tools/lib/vendor_licenses.py`
# enforces it: an `install` naming an undeclared `source` fails, a `source` no
# `install` uses fails, a `sha256` that does not match the file on disk fails,
# and an `install` whose vendor directory is absent from the checkout fails as
# a stale declaration rather than being skipped. A crate that starts shipping
# its own licence therefore surfaces as a stale record to delete, which is the
# only way this file gets smaller.
#
# ADDING ONE
#
# Do not write the text. Fetch it from the crate's `repository` URL at a commit
# you can name, check that commit predates or matches the vendored release,
# record the sha256 you actually measured, and add both records.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sources
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# convert_case 0.4.0 — MIT. The crate's `authors` field is
# `David Purdum <purdum41@gmail.com>`, which is who this text names; the
# repository's later copy (commit e58d5ae, 2025) renames the holder to
# "rutrum" and is the wrong artifact for a 2021 release. The pinned commit is
# the one that added the file in 2020 and is what the repository held when
# 0.4.0 was published.
source  convert-case/LICENSE  797087750c4103075e96bb7a60202a040812f8679ae2f7263148a1cc0b298d28  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rutrum/convert-case/f72ca63c9d579fbab22e361c76e39d31d1e86a2e/LICENSE

# rust-phf — MIT, one repository for all eight vendored phf crates. Pinned at
# the 2016 revision rather than the current one: the file was next touched in
# July 2022 ("Update copyright year", which also adds a second holder), and
# both vendored releases — 0.8.0 (2019) and 0.10.x (2021) — predate that.
source  rust-phf/LICENSE  d8870d5e2ab20fc50915b711c5aa7588131c9abcc1bcb980da1efd7486522a46  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rust-phf/rust-phf/e7cffb04585e5fa25b81b58d3b1b1605a239f253/LICENSE

# seahash 3.0.7 — MIT. Upstream is GitLab, not GitHub. The licence file was
# added in 2023 ("fix: add missing MIT license text"), after the 3.0.7 release
# that is vendored here, and is the only licence text the project has ever
# published; there is no earlier revision to prefer.
source  seahash/LICENSE  23f18e03dc49df91622fe2a76176497404e46ced8a715d9d2b67a7446571cca3  https://gitlab.redox-os.org/redox-os/seahash/-/raw/3088c5c912b70b586d27bf553fbe964e025a2c89/LICENSE

# selectors 0.24.0 — MPL-2.0. The crate is published out of the Servo
# monorepo, whose root LICENSE is the MPL 2.0 text; it has not changed since
# 2018, well before the vendored release.
source  servo/LICENSE  1af2d2b02d9c86030d29ed77117ca7a1b0e4b6ff35bcf8eeb124867d0f5cb59e  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/servo/servo/f5ab95786f9cbbf5804d87f265812325fc80ad7b/LICENSE

# The Apache License 2.0, verbatim from the Apache Software Foundation, for the
# two dual-licensed crates gnrt resolves to Apache-2.0.
#
# fxhash 0.2.1 declares `Apache-2.0/MIT` and its repository has never contained
# a licence file of any kind, so there is no upstream artifact to take and the
# canonical document is the only truthful option. rust-siphash does now publish
# `LICENSE-APACHE` (added June 2026, after the vendored 0.3.11), and that file
# is BYTE-IDENTICAL to this one — verified, same sha256 — so one copy serves
# both rather than two identical files pretending to be different evidence.
source  apache-2.0/LICENSE-APACHE  cfc7749b96f63bd31c3c42b5c471bf756814053e847c10f3eb003417bc523d30  https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Installs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

install  convert_case-v0_4      LICENSE         convert-case/LICENSE

install  phf-v0_8               LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf-v0_10              LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf_codegen-v0_8       LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf_generator-v0_8     LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf_generator-v0_10    LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf_macros-v0_10       LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf_shared-v0_8        LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE
install  phf_shared-v0_10       LICENSE         rust-phf/LICENSE

install  seahash-v3             LICENSE         seahash/LICENSE

install  selectors-v0_24        LICENSE         servo/LICENSE

install  fxhash-v0_2            LICENSE-APACHE  apache-2.0/LICENSE-APACHE
install  siphasher-v0_3         LICENSE-APACHE  apache-2.0/LICENSE-APACHE
